  "mn135:3.5": "",
  "mn135:3.6": "",
  "mn135:3.7": "",
  "mn135:3.8": "",
  "mn135:3.9": "",
  "mn135:4.1": "This phrase is also explained at <a href='https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato'>AN 10.216</a>. In <a href='https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato'>AN 5.57</a> it is one of five sayings that should be frequently recollected by “a woman or a man, a layperson or a renunciate”. ",
  "mn135:4.2": "",
  "mn135:4.3": "",
  "mn135:4.4": "",
  "mn135:4.5": "",
  "mn135:4.6": "",
  "mn135:4.7": "",
  "mn135:4.8": "",
  "mn135:5.1": "The phrase “some woman or man” is used only here and in a few suttas that also deal with kamma (<a href='https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato'>AN 3.70</a>, <a href='https://suttacentral.net/an8.42/en/sujato'>AN 8.42</a>, <a href='https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato'>AN 8.43</a>, <a href='https://suttacentral.net/an8.45/en/sujato'>AN 8.45</a>). The mention of “woman or man” undercuts assumptions that women lack agency, or that their rebirth is lesser. ",
  "mn135:5.2": "",
  "mn135:5.3": "Rebirth in the human realm is always due to good deeds in the past, but while in this realm, bad kamma can take effect. Of course, not all afflictions are caused by past kamma, a doctrine of the Jains that the Buddha rejected. ",
  "mn135:5.4": "",
  "mn135:5.5": "",
